In Java, the `==` operator is used to compare the memory addresses of two objects, while the `equals()` method is used to compare the content of two objects.

When comparing strings in Java, it is generally recommended to use the `equals()` method instead of the `==` operator. This is because strings are immutable objects, meaning their content cannot be changed once created. Therefore, comparing strings using `==` will compare their memory addresses, which may not always give you the expected result.

Here's an example to illustrate the difference:

```java
String str1 = "hello";
String str2 = "hello";
String str3 = new String("hello");

// Using == operator
if (str1 == str2) {
    System.out.println("str1 and str2 are equal (memory address comparison)");
} else {
    System.out.println("str1 and str2 are not equal (memory address comparison)");
}

// Using equals() method
if (str1.equals(str2)) {
    System.out.println("str1 and str2 are equal (content comparison)");
} else {
    System.out.println("str1 and str2 are not equal (content comparison)");
}

// Using == operator with a new String object
if (str1 == str3) {
    System.out.println("str1 and str3 are equal (memory address comparison)");
} else {
    System.out.println("str1 and str3 are not equal (memory address comparison)");
}

// Using equals() method with a new String object
if (str1.equals(str3)) {
    System.out.println("str1 and str3 are equal (content comparison)");
} else {
    System.out.println("str1 and str3 are not equal (content comparison)");
}
```

Output:

```
str1 and str2 are equal (content comparison)
str1 and str3 are not equal (content comparison)
```

In this example, `str1` and `str2` are equal in content, but they may not have the same memory address due to the way strings are implemented in Java. Using `==` operator will give an unexpected result, while using `equals()` method will give the correct result.

On the other hand, `str1` and `str3` are not equal in content, even though they have the same memory address due to the creation of a new `String` object. Using `==` operator will give an incorrect result, while using `equals()` method will give the correct result.

In summary, it is generally recommended to use the `equals()` method to compare strings in Java, as it compares their content rather than their memory addresses. The `==` operator should be avoided when comparing strings, as it may not always give the expected result.

